---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="Workflows"
   summary="Step-by-step guides for the most common accessibility testing tasks. Each workflow shows the commands to run and when to use them."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="check-one-criterion">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Workflow 1</p>
            <h2>Check one criterion</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            Use this when you know which WCAG rule to test, or when a bug report references a specific
            criterion.
         </p>
         <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js wcag show 4.1.3 --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js inspect criterion 4.1.3 --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js run pattern status_message_probe --target virtual --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js verify criterion 4.1.3 --target virtual --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">see also</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            If you don't know the exact rule, start with <a href="/criterion-lookup">criterion
            lookup</a> or <a href="/applicability">applicability</a>.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="verify-a-level">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Workflow 2</p>
            <h2>Verify a target level</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            Use this when you need to verify A, AA, or AAA conformance for a target.
            The <code>verify level</code> command expands the full criterion matrix and runs each
            applicable check.
         </p>
         <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js verify level AA --target virtual --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/basic-page.html --json</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">important</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Read <a href="/verification-semantics">verification semantics</a> to understand how
            mixed results, manual-only rows, and uncovered criteria appear in the output.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="drive-a-session">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Workflow 3</p>
            <h2>Drive a session manually</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            Use the driver when you need direct control over the screen reader — navigating elements,
            sending keystrokes, and reading spoken output.
         </p>
         <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js drive start --target virtual --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/basic-page.html --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js drive next --session &lt;sessionId&gt; --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js drive read --session &lt;sessionId&gt; --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js drive stop --session &lt;sessionId&gt; --json</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">note</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Driver transcripts capture what the screen reader said. To make a conformance claim,
            follow up with the <a href="/verification-semantics">verification</a> layer.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="agent-and-storybook">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Workflow 4</p>
            <h2>Use it from an agent or Storybook</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            The MCP server exposes a11ied to AI agents and editors. The Storybook integration lets you
            test individual components without a full app.
         </p>
         <div class="workflow-list">
            <div class="workflow-row">
               <strong><a href="/mcp-usage">MCP usage</a></strong>
               <span>Tool calls and read-only resources for editors and AI agents.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-row">
               <strong><a href="/storybook-usage">Storybook usage</a></strong>
               <span>Test Storybook stories as accessibility targets.</span>
            </div>
         </div>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">tip</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            For read-only queries like criterion lookup, use the knowledge tools first — they don't
            need a browser.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Guidance</p>
         <h2>Use only the layers you need</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Each layer serves a specific purpose. A criterion lookup doesn't require a browser.
         A driver session doesn't produce a compliance verdict. Use the narrowest tool that
         answers your question.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
